+A mathematical reasoning model can become more accurate while losing
+alternative correct solutions. To measure this loss, we introduce \mb{},
+a benchmark of five executable domains that distinguishes different correct
+outputs for the same problem. We then show that ReplayMaxRL, which combines
+sampling-aware reinforcement learning with a memory of previously verified
+outputs, improves held-out correctness while preserving a broader repertoire.
+We find gains from replay with both Dr.GRPO and MaxRL, across three and two
+model scales, respectively, and on a harder graph-coloring task. These results
+distinguish discovering a successful behavior from keeping it available.
 \end{abstract}
 
